# Answer 2 · Which Feminism Can Raise Women's Status in Pakistan? (CSS 2018)
@py question 2018-4

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note "Type(s)" invites more than one answer; match each school to a dimension of status and end with a layered, home-grown model.
@ask WHICH TYPE(S) of feminism
@ask can ELEVATE women's SOCIO-CULTURAL, ECONOMIC and POLITICAL status in PAKISTAN?
(1) Introduction: feminism is a family of theories; Pakistan ranks 143rd of 145; no single school is enough
(2) The status gap: 24.4% of women in the labour force; 53% female literacy; 11 women won general NA seats
(3) Liberal feminism raises legal and political status: Articles 25, 27, 34; laws of 2010–2025; quotas
(4) Islamic feminism raises socio-cultural status: inheritance, mahr, khula and consent argued from the Qur'an
(5) Socialist feminism raises economic status: unpaid care, home-based workers, Lady Health Workers, BISP
(6) Radical insight protects safety: violence controls women; laws on acid, honour killing and rape
(7) Men's feminism engages the gatekeepers: fathers, husbands, clerics and police decide most women's choices
(8) Intersectionality decides who comes first: rural, poor, minority and disabled women and khawaja siras
(9) The less suitable schools: pure radical, Marxist, postmodern and psychoanalytic — and what each still offers
(10) Conclusion: a plural, home-grown feminism — the way Pakistan's own reforms were won
:::

## Decoding the question
**"What type(s)"** signals that the examiner accepts — even expects — **more than one** school. The question names **three dimensions** of status: **socio-cultural, economic and political**. The best answer **matches each school to a dimension**, gives Pakistani evidence for each, admits the limits of each, and ends with a **combined model**. The trap is to write a history of the women's movement (that is a different question) or to back one school and dismiss the rest.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Feminism** is the belief in **equal rights and chances** for women and men, with **action** to achieve it. It is not one ideology but a **family of theories** — liberal, radical, socialist, Islamic, intersectional and others — which differ on the **cause** of women's disadvantage and its **cure**.

#### ii) The context
Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== countries in the World Economic Forum's **Global Gender Gap Report 2026**, with only ==59.5%== of its gender gap closed.

#### iii) The argument
No single imported school can raise women's status on all three fronts. Each school fits **one dimension** best. Pakistan needs a **plural, home-grown feminism** that combines them.

### 2. The status gap in three dimensions

::: table | Women's status in Pakistan: the three dimensions named in the question.
@widths 3,6,5
Dimension | Evidence | The school that fits best
Political and legal | Only ==11== women won general National Assembly seats in ==2024==; presence rests on ==60== reserved seats; GGGR political rank ==107th== | Liberal
Socio-cultural | Female literacy about ==53%== against about ==68%== for men (Census ==2023==); honour crimes; child marriage | Islamic, radical insight
Economic | Female labour-force participation ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25); GGGR economic rank ==144th== | Socialist
:::

### 3. Liberal feminism — the legal and political floor

#### i) What it offers
**Liberal feminism** seeks **equal rights, laws and opportunities** within the existing system. It is the natural language of Pakistan's **Constitution of 1973**: **Article 25** (no discrimination on the basis of sex), **Article 27** (no sex discrimination in public service) and **Article 34** (full participation of women in national life).

#### ii) What it has achieved
Laws on **workplace harassment** (==2010==), **honour killing** (==2016==), **rape** (==2021==) and the **Islamabad Child Marriage Restraint Act** (==2025==, minimum age ==18==); the **60 reserved seats**; the **National Commission on the Status of Women** (==2000==).

#### iii) Its limit
Good laws are **weakly enforced**, and poverty and custom stop most women from using them. A village woman cannot use the harassment law if she cannot reach a court or pay a lawyer. Liberal feminism supplies the **floor**, not the whole building.

### 4. Islamic feminism — socio-cultural legitimacy

#### i) What it offers
**Islamic feminism** seeks gender justice by **re-reading the Qur'an and Sunnah**, separating the divine message from patriarchal custom. Thinkers include **Amina Wadud** (*Qur'an and Woman*, ==1992==) and the Pakistani-born scholars **Riffat Hassan** and **Asma Barlas** (==2002==).

#### ii) Why it fits Pakistan
In a society where most people see life through faith, rights argued from Islam — **inheritance**, ***mahr***, ***khula***, **consent to marriage**, **education** — meet far less resistance than rights argued from foreign charters. Many women are denied inheritance by **custom**, not by religion.

#### iii) Its limit
Secular feminists, such as **Afiya Zia** (*Faith and Feminism in Pakistan*, ==2018==), fear it limits how far reform can go; some conservatives call it a Western agenda in religious dress. It works best **alongside** constitutional rights, not in place of them.

#### iv) An example
The **Muslim Family Laws Ordinance** (==1961==) required marriages to be registered and restricted polygamy — a reform defended in Islamic terms, and one that has survived for more than sixty years.

### 5. Socialist feminism — economic status

#### i) What it offers
**Socialist feminism** sees **capitalism and patriarchy** working together. It makes **unpaid care** and **low-paid women's work** visible.

#### ii) Pakistani evidence
Most employed women work in **agriculture**, much as **unpaid family helpers**. Millions of **home-based workers** stitch and embroider for piece rates — **Sindh** passed Pakistan's first **Home-Based Workers Act** in ==2018==. The **Lady Health Workers**' long struggle for regular jobs is a socialist-feminist struggle.

#### iii) Policies it suggests
**Childcare**, **safe transport**, **equal pay**, labour rights for domestic and home-based workers, and **cash transfers to women** such as the **Benazir Income Support Programme** (==2008==).

### 6. Radical insight — safety and bodily integrity

#### i) The insight
**Radical feminism** (Millett, ==1970==) showed that **violence** — rape, battering, harassment — is a tool that keeps women under control.

#### ii) In Pakistan
The **Women's Action Forum**'s fight against the **Hudood Ordinances** (==1979==) and laws on **acid attacks** (==2011==), **honour killing** and **rape** reflect this insight. Its **analysis** is vital; its most provocative **slogans** can produce backlash, as the controversy over the **Aurat March** slogan *Mera Jism Meri Marzi* (==2019==–20) showed.

#### iii) How to use it
Argue the radical demand — **freedom from violence and control** — in the language of the Constitution (**Article 14**, the dignity of man) and of Islam's protection of life and honour. The substance then wins broad support.

### 7. Men's feminism — engaging the gatekeepers

#### i) Why men matter
Most decisions about a Pakistani woman's schooling, marriage, mobility and inheritance are made by **fathers, brothers, husbands and elders**.

#### ii) What works
Engaging **fathers** for girls' schooling, **religious scholars** for consent and inheritance, and **police** for gender-sensitive handling of complaints. Organisations such as **Rozan** have long worked with boys, men and police. UN Women's **HeForShe** campaign (==2014==) carries the same idea internationally.

#### iii) Why this is feminist, not a concession
**bell hooks** argued that feminism is against **sexism**, not men. Winning men over is not surrender — it is how change passes the **family gate**.

### 8. Intersectionality — deciding who comes first

#### i) The tool
**Intersectionality** (**Kimberlé Crenshaw**, ==1989==) shows that gender overlaps with **class, place, religion, ethnicity and disability**.

#### ii) In Pakistan
A professional woman in Islamabad and a landless woman in Tharparkar are both "women", but their needs differ. Policy must reach **rural, poor, minority and disabled women** and ***khawaja siras*** first, not only the urban middle class.

::: fig pyramid | A layered feminism for Pakistan, from the foundation up.
Foundation: constitutional equality — liberal feminism
Legitimacy: rights argued from the faith — Islamic feminism
Livelihood: work, pay and care — socialist feminism
Safety: freedom from violence — radical insight
Reach: the most marginalised first — intersectionality
:::

### 9. The less suitable schools

#### i) Pure radical and Marxist feminism
**Separatist** radical feminism provokes backlash and is easily painted as foreign. **Marxist** feminism waits for a revolution that is not coming; its insight on unpaid work survives better through socialist policy.

#### ii) Postmodern and psychoanalytic feminism
**Postmodern** feminism is too abstract for policy, but it warns against speaking of "the Pakistani woman" as one person. **Psychoanalytic** feminism offers no quick policy, but its call for **shared parenting** matters for the long term.

### 10. Conclusion
The answer to "which type(s)?" is **all the useful parts of several**: **liberal** in law, **Islamic** in cultural justification, **socialist** in economics, **radical** in its refusal to tolerate violence, **inclusive of men**, and **intersectional** in whom it reaches first. This is not a compromise; it is how Pakistan's own successful reforms were won — from the **Muslim Family Laws Ordinance** (==1961==) to the honour-killing law of ==2016==, each combined constitutional rights, Islamic argument and women's activism.

## Why this answer scores
- It answers **"type(s)"** literally — several schools, each matched to one of the **three dimensions** named in the question.
- A **table** of Pakistani data and a **pyramid** figure make the argument visible.
- Each school has **what it offers → Pakistani evidence → its limit**, so the answer is balanced.
- The **less suitable schools** are dealt with fairly, not ignored.
- The conclusion ties the model to **Pakistan's own reform history**, answering the "Western agenda" objection.
